+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: priority-queue 2.8.0
+
+Property-based tests written with [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`hegeltest` 0.28.2),
+added to the existing integration test files:
+
+- `tests/priority_queue.rs` — modules `hegel_pqueue_tests` and `hegel_serde_tests`
+- `tests/double_priority_queue.rs` — module `hegel_doublepq_tests`
+
+Note: `hegeltest` was added with `cargo add --dev hegeltest --ignore-rust-version`
+because its MSRV (1.86) is newer than the crate's `rust-version = "1.65.0"`.
+This only affects dev-dependencies; whether that is acceptable upstream is a
+maintainer decision.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+### PriorityQueue (`tests/priority_queue.rs`)
+
+1. **`pq_agrees_with_hashmap_model`** (stateful model test). A
+   `PriorityQueue<i32, i64>` must agree with a `HashMap<i32, i64>` reference
+   model under arbitrary interleavings of `push`, `pop`, `change_priority`,
+   `push_increase`, `push_decrease`, `remove`, `get`/`get_priority`/`contains`.
+   Invariants after every step: `len`/`is_empty` agree with the model, and
+   `peek` returns an item whose priority equals the model maximum (ties are
+   resolved by looking the popped/peeked item up in the model rather than
+   predicting the winner). Evidence: each rule asserts exactly the documented
+   return value of the corresponding method (e.g. `push` returns the replaced
+   priority, `change_priority` returns the old priority or `None`).
+
+2. **`pop_drains_in_non_increasing_priority_order`**. After up to 300 unique
+   pushes followed by up to 50 `change_priority` calls on random existing keys
+   (exercising `up_heapify`/`bubble_up` deep in the heap), a full drain via
+   `pop` yields priorities in non-increasing order and returns every element
+   exactly once. Evidence: `pop` is documented to remove "the item with the
+   greatest priority".
+
+3. **`into_sorted_vec_sorted_by_priority`**. `into_sorted_vec` returns every
+   pushed item exactly once, ordered from highest associated priority to
+   lowest (checked against a `HashMap` of expected priorities). Evidence: doc
+   comment "Returns a `Vec<I>` sorted from the item associated to the highest
+   priority to the lowest".
+
+4. **`push_increase_takes_max`**. On an arbitrary queue, after
+   `push_increase(k, p)` the stored priority is `max(old, p)`; return value is
+   `None` when absent, `Some(old)` when increased, `Some(p)` when not.
+   Evidence: the method's doc comment and doctest spell out exactly this
+   contract.
+
+5. **`push_decrease_takes_min`**. Mirror property for `push_decrease`
+   (stored priority becomes `min(old, p)`, same return-value scheme).
+
+6. **`iter_yields_exact_contents`**. For up to 300 unique-key pushes, `iter`
+   visits exactly the pushed `(item, priority)` pairs, each once (order is
+   documented as arbitrary, so the check is multiset equality via count +
+   `HashMap` equality).
+
+7. **`from_iter_equals_push_loop`**. A queue collected via `FromIterator`
+   from a pair sequence *with duplicate keys allowed* equals (by `PartialEq`,
+   which compares contents) a queue built by pushing every pair in order —
+   the source documents that for repeated items "the item inside the pq is
+   updated", i.e. last pair wins, same as `push`. The `FromIterator`-built
+   queue (which uses the O(N) `heap_build` path instead of per-push
+   `bubble_up`) must also drain in non-increasing priority order.
+
+8. **`serde_json_roundtrip_preserves_queue`** (gated on `--features serde`).
+   `serde_json::from_str(serde_json::to_string(pq))` yields a queue equal to
+   the original, and the deserialized queue's heap is valid: a full drain via
+   `pop` is sorted and returns all elements. Evidence: the crate ships
+   `Serialize`/`Deserialize` impls that rebuild the heap on deserialization.
+
+### DoublePriorityQueue (`tests/double_priority_queue.rs`)
+
+9. **`double_pq_agrees_with_hashmap_model`** (stateful model test). Same
+   scheme as property 1 for the min-max ("interval") heap: rules `push`,
+   `pop_min`, `pop_max`, `change_priority`, `remove`, `get`; invariants that
+   `len` agrees and that `peek_min`/`peek_max` return the model
+   minimum/maximum after every step.
+
+10. **`interleaved_pop_min_pop_max_agree_with_model`**. Build a
+    DoublePriorityQueue with up to 300 unique keys, then perform up to 50
+    random `change_priority`/`remove` operations while the heap is deep, then
+    fully drain it with an arbitrary interleaving of `pop_min`/`pop_max`,
+    asserting each pop returns exactly the current min/max priority of the
+    remaining elements (ties resolved through the model). This targets the
+    stale-heap-index / min-max-level bugs that only appear several levels deep.
+
+11. **`sorted_vecs_agree_with_reference_sort`**. With all-distinct priorities
+    (making the order unique), `into_ascending_sorted_vec` equals the items
+    sorted by priority via `sort_unstable` on `(priority, item)` pairs, and
+    `into_descending_sorted_vec` is exactly its reverse. Evidence: the two
+    methods' doc comments ("sorted from the one associated to the lowest
+    priority to the highest" and vice versa).
+
+### Generator notes
+
+- Priorities are drawn from the **full `i64` range** (no arithmetic is done on
+  them, so boundary values like `i64::MIN`/`MAX` are pure signal).
+- Model-machine keys mix a small `0..=15` range (so duplicate pushes, priority
+  changes and removals of *existing* items happen often) with the full `i32`
+  range, via `one_of!`.
+- Batch tests draw a size up to 300 separately and use `min_size` so heaps get
+  hundreds of elements / 8+ levels deep, per the skill's large-collection
+  guidance. Keys use `.unique(true)` so multiset accounting is unambiguous.
+
+## Test results
+
+- `cargo test`: all 105 tests pass (55 in double_priority_queue.rs including
+  3 hegel tests, 53 in priority_queue.rs including 7 hegel tests, 17 doctests).
+- `cargo test --features serde`: all pass, including the hegel serde
+  round-trip property (11 hegel properties total).
+- **Liveness check**: two properties were temporarily inverted
+  (`pop_drains...` order check, and the `pop_min` model check). Both failed
+  immediately and hegel shrank the model-machine failure to the minimal
+  counterexample `push(0,0); push(1,1); pop_min` — confirming the tests are
+  live and shrinking works. The breaks were reverted and the `.hegel/` failure
+  database removed.
+- **Exploratory 10x run**: all 11 properties were run once with
+  `test_cases = 1000` (10x the default). All passed (~40s total). Committed
+  tests use the default count.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+None found. Two areas looked suspicious from reading the source and were
+targeted specifically, but survived the model tests and the 10x run:
+
+- The asymmetry in `src/double_priority_queue/mod.rs` between `pop_min_if`
+  (restores with `heapify`) and `pop_max_if` (restores with `up_heapify`),
+  and the `heapify`-only restoration in `pop_max`, where the leaf swapped
+  into a max-level position could conceivably need to move toward the root.
+  The grandchild/parent swap inside `heapify_min`/`heapify_max` appears to
+  handle this correctly.
+- `Store::remove`'s four-way index-fixup logic (`src/store.rs`), classic
+  stale-index territory, exercised by the `remove` rules and the deep-heap
+  interleaved test.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+None were committed and none needed to be abandoned mid-way. Two properties
+were deliberately *designed around* documented non-guarantees rather than
+asserted naively:
+
+- Pop/peek do **not** promise which item wins among equal priorities, so all
+  model comparisons check the returned priority against the model extreme and
+  then look the returned *item* up in the model, instead of predicting the
+  item.
+- `into_ascending_sorted_vec` vs reversed `into_descending_sorted_vec`
+  equality is only sound with distinct priorities (equal-priority items may
+  legitimately come out in different relative orders), so that test generates
+  priorities with `.unique(true)`.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The Rust reference's Setup section predicting the MSRV `cargo add` failure
+  (and the `--ignore-rust-version` fix) was exactly right and saved time.
+- The stateful testing API (`#[hegel::state_machine]`, `#[rule]`,
+  `#[invariant]`, `hegel::stateful::run`) worked first try as documented;
+  the note that invariants run on the initial (empty) state mattered — both
+  peek invariants had to handle the empty queue.
+- The "draw size separately, then `min_size(n)`" pattern for large collections
+  and the `.unique(true)` key guidance mapped directly onto this crate's needs.
+- Shrinking quality was excellent: the deliberately-broken DPQ model shrank to
+  a 3-step, 2-element counterexample with verbose step output.
+- Minor friction: there is no global case-count override (no `PROPTEST_CASES`
+  equivalent), so the 10x exploratory run required a temporary `sed` over the
+  test attributes, as the reference itself suggests.
